    "question": {
      "math_academy_id": "q-88830",
      "knowledge_point_id": "c07eb336-1ac8-5633-abaf-fcfca4c12bd1",
      "knowledge_point": "Using The Law of Total Probability Stated in Terms of Intersections",
      "problem": "In a class, $17$ students are boys, and $23$ are girls. Of the boys, $2$ belong to the school chess club. Of the girls, $3$ are in the chess club. What is the probability that a randomly selected student in the class is in the chess club?",
      "worked_solution": "Let $C$ be the event that the chosen student is on the chess club, and let $B$ be the event that the student is a boy. Then ${B}^{′}$ is the event that the student is a girl.\n\nTo calculate the probability that a randomly selected student is on the chess club, we can use the law of total probability:\n\n$$\nP(C)=P(C\\cap B)+P(C\\cap {B}^{′})\n$$\n\nTo calculate $P(C\\cap B),$ we take the number of students in the class who are on the chess club and are boys $(2)$ and divide by the total number of students in the class $(17+23=40).$ So, we have\n\n$$\nP(C\\cap B)=\\frac{2}{40}.\n$$\n\nSimilarly, to calculate $P(C\\cap {B}^{′}),$ we take the number of students in the class who are on the chess club and are girls $(3)$ and divide by the total number of students in the class $(40).$ So, we have\n\n$$\nP(C\\cap {B}^{′})=\\frac{3}{40}.\n$$\n\nFinally, substituting the above information into the law of total probability, the probability that a randomly selected student is on the chess club is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P(C) & =P(C\\cap B)+P(C\\cap {B}^{′}) \\\\  & =\\frac{2}{40}+\\frac{3}{40} \\\\  & =\\frac{5}{40} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{8}.\\end{aligned}\n$$",
      "answer_fields": [
