      "math_academy_id": "q-88892",
      "knowledge_point_id": "c07eb336-1ac8-5633-abaf-fcfca4c12bd1",
      "knowledge_point": "Using The Law of Total Probability Stated in Terms of Intersections",
      "problem": "Alice has two email accounts, $A$ and $B.$ In account $A,$ she received $24$ emails of which $5$ are spam. In the account $B,$ she received $18$ emails of which $2$ are spam. What is the probability that a randomly selected email in either account is spam?",
      "worked_solution": "Let $S$ be the event that the chosen email is spam, and let $A$ be the event that the email was received in account $A.$ Then ${A}^{′}$ is the event that the email was received in account $B.$\n\nTo calculate the probability that a randomly selected email is spam, we can use the law of total probability:\n\n$$\nP(S)=P(S\\cap A)+P(S\\cap {A}^{′})\n$$\n\nTo calculate $P(S\\cap A),$ we take the number of emails in account A that are spam $(5)$ and divide by the total number of emails received $(24+18=42).$ So, we have\n\n$$\nP(S\\cap A)=\\frac{5}{42}.\n$$\n\nSimilarly, to calculate $P(S\\cap {A}^{′}),$ we take the number of emails in account $B$ that are spam $(2)$ and divide by the total number of emails received $(24+18=42).$ So, we have\n\n$$\nP(S\\cap {A}^{′})=\\frac{2}{42}.\n$$\n\nFinally, substituting the above information into the law of total probability, the probability that a randomly selected email is spam is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P(S) & =P(S\\cap A)+P(S\\cap {A}^{′}) \\\\  & =\\frac{5}{42}+\\frac{2}{42} \\\\  & =\\frac{7}{42} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{6}.\\end{aligned}\n$$",
